              "text": "In plastic welding\u2014specifically **hot gas plastic welding**\u2014nitrogen is used as a shielding gas to replace regular compressed air. \n\nUsing nitrogen instead of standard air is essential for several reasons:\n\n### 1. It Prevents Oxidation (Burning)\nStandard ambient air contains roughly 21% oxygen. When plastics are heated to their melting points in the presence of oxygen, they rapidly oxidize. This causes the plastic to literally burn, char, and degrade at the molecular level. \nBecause **nitrogen is an inert gas**, using it to heat and push the plastic flushes oxygen completely out of the weld zone. Without oxygen, the plastic cannot burn or oxidize.\n\n### 2. It Creates Significantly Stronger Welds\nWhen plastic oxidizes during a weld, its polymer chains break down, and impurities (like carbon, ash, and soot) get trapped in the joint. This results in a weak, brittle weld that is highly prone to cracking. \nBy shielding the weld zone with nitrogen, the plastic and the filler rod melt cleanly and fuse together at a molecular level without contamination. This produces a highly flexible, structural weld that is as close as possible to the strength of the original plastic.\n\n### 3. No Smoke, Ash, or Sparks\nWhen welding with standard hot air, the combustion of the plastic generates smoke, soot, and sometimes even sparks. \nUsing nitrogen eliminates combustion. As a result, there is **virtually no smoke or harmful fumes** emitted during the welding process, making it a much safer and cleaner environment for the technician.\n\n### 4. Better Aesthetics and Easier Refinishing\nBecause the plastic does not char, burn, or discolor, the finished weld is incredibly clean. This is especially important in automotive repairs (such as fixing cracked bumper covers). A clean weld without charred black spots is much easier to scrape, sand, prime, and paint.\n\n### 5. Highly Critical for Certain Plastics\nWhile nitrogen benefits all thermoplastics, it is absolutely essential for **polyolefins** like **Polyethylene (PE)** and **Polypropylene (PP/TPO)**. These plastics (commonly used in automotive bumpers, gas tanks, water tanks, and kayak bodies) are highly sensitive to heat and oxygen and will rapidly degrade and fail if welded with standard compressed air. \n\n---\n\n### How Nitrogen Plastic Welders Work in Practice\nTo prevent wasting nitrogen gas, most professional plastic welding systems are designed to run on cheap **compressed air** while the heating element is warming up or idling. The machine only switches to **nitrogen** when the technician pulls the trigger to start the actual weld. Many modern shops use either compressed nitrogen tanks or machines with built-in nitrogen generators that extract nitrogen directly from the workshop's compressed air lines.",
